        "content": "Now let me solve the problem carefully.\n\n**Part (1):** In △ABC, AB=3, BC=2√3, cosB=√3/3.\n\nBy law of cosines: AC² = AB² + BC² − 2·AB·BC·cosB = 9 + 12 − 2·3·2√3·(√3/3) = 21 − 12 = 9. So AC = 3.\n\nThen cosA = (AB² + AC² − BC²)/(2·AB·AC) = (9 + 9 − 12)/(2·3·3) = 6/18 = 1/3.\n\n**Part (2):** D on ray BA beyond A, DE∥BC, AE⊥AC, DE=√6, find CE.\n\nLet me set up coordinates. Place A at origin, AC along x-axis. Since AC=3, C=(3,0). cosA=1/3, sinA=2√2/3. AB=3, so B=(3·cosA, 3·sinA)=(1, 2√2).\n\nCheck BC: from B(1,2√2) to C(3,0): √(4+8)=√12=2√3 ✓.\n\nD on ray BA beyond A: ray from B through A extended. Direction A−B = (−1,−2√2). D = A + t(A−B) = (−t, −2√2 t), t>0.\n\nDE∥BC: BC direction = C−B = (2, −2√2). So E = D + s(2,−2√2) = (−t+2s, −2√2 t − 2√2 s).\n\nAE⊥AC: AC is x-axis, so AE⊥AC means E has x-coordinate 0: −t+2s=0 → s = t/2.\n\nDE = √6: |DE| = |s|·|BC| = (t/2)·2√3 = t√3 = √6 → t = √2.\n\nSo s = √2/2. E = (0, −2√2·√2 − 2√2·(√2/2)) = (0, −4 − 2) = (0, −6).\n\nCE = distance from C(3,0) to E(0,−6) = √(9+36) = √45 = 3√5.\n\nLet me verify with the tool submission.",
